"Evaluates business units using composite industry attractiveness and competitive strength rather than growth and share alone.\n\nUse when: Allocate attention across a diverse business portfolio. Make portfolio criteria and their weighting open to review.\n\nAvoid when: The team cannot justify the criteria, ratings or aggregation behind the axis positions.\n\nVisual grammar: A three-by-three grid crosses industry attractiveness with competitive strength. Business-unit markers occupy scored positions. Supporting scorecards explain the composite axes and any bubble-size measure.\n\nBuild: Define portfolio units and meaningful market boundaries. Agree criteria and weights separately for each composite axis. Score units, place them and discuss investment options with sensitivity checks.\n\nCheck: Attractiveness and strength use distinct, nonredundant criteria. Ratings share defined anchors and a common review period. Zone guidance is considered alongside strategic dependencies.\n\nPitfalls: Hiding subjective judgments behind apparently precise coordinates. Substituting market share for the entire competitive-strength assessment.\n\nExample: A services group compares sectors using demand quality and profitability, then assesses each unit's capabilities and customer position.\n\nAlternatives: BCG matrix, Weighted decision matrix."
